+// Klepton: variable rasterization rate (foveated rendering).
+//
+// Upstream ANGLE has no notion of MTLRasterizationRateMap. Klepton renders the
+// guest's eye textures into MTLTextures it allocates itself, and wants those
+// passes rasterized through a rate map so the periphery costs fewer fragments.
+//
+// The association is kept OUT of mtl::Texture on purpose: a Texture is shared
+// with its views and its UsageRef, is built by nine different constructors, and
+// nothing in ANGLE would carry the map across a view. A side registry keyed on
+// the underlying id<MTLTexture> has none of that surface, and the lookup happens
+// once per render pass build, not per draw.
+//
+// Registered from outside ANGLE via the exported C entry points at the bottom of
+// mtl_common.mm; with nothing registered every path below is one atomic load.
+id<MTLRasterizationRateMap> GetRasterizationRateMapFor(id<MTLTexture> texture);
+
+// A null map unbinds. Both the texture and the map are retained while bound —
+// see the implementation for why the TEXTURE has to be.
+void SetRasterizationRateMapFor(id<MTLTexture> texture, id<MTLRasterizationRateMap> map);
+
+// The same thing keyed on a render target's DIMENSIONS instead of its identity.
+//
+// This exists because the target that actually matters cannot be named from
+// outside ANGLE. The guest's expensive pass renders into a multisampled
+// RENDERBUFFER that ANGLE allocates itself in response to
+// glRenderbufferStorageMultisample — there is no MTLTexture for the runtime to
+// hold, and no GL object it can hand us either without dragging gl::Context into
+// the Metal backend. Its size, though, is exactly the eye size, and so is the
+// eye texture's, which is precisely the pair that must share one map for the
+// resolve between them to stay a physical-to-physical copy.
+//
+// Checked only when the per-texture lookup misses.
+// `minSamples` qualifies the rule, and is not optional in practice. The guest
+// allocates OTHER render targets at the eye size — a post-processing chain reads
+// and writes full-resolution intermediates — and foveating one of those is a
+// wrong picture, because the guest samples it with screen-space coordinates that
+// know nothing about the warp. The scene target is distinguishable from all of
+// them by being MULTISAMPLED, so a rule of minSamples=2 hits it and nothing
+// else. Pass 1 to match any render target of that size.
+void SetRasterizationRateMapForSize(uint32_t width,
+                                    uint32_t height,
+                                    uint32_t minSamples,
+                                    id<MTLRasterizationRateMap> map);
+id<MTLRasterizationRateMap> GetRasterizationRateMapForSize(uint32_t width,
+                                                           uint32_t height,
+                                                           uint32_t samples);
+
+// Both keys, under ONE lock. This is the accessor render passes and blits
+// should use: the two-call form takes the registry mutex twice per render
+// target, and once anything is registered that is paid on every framebuffer
+// state sync the guest performs — not just on the two targets the feature is
+// actually about.
+id<MTLRasterizationRateMap> GetRasterizationRateMapForTarget(id<MTLTexture> texture,
+                                                             uint32_t width,
+                                                             uint32_t height,
+                                                             uint32_t samples);
+
+// Fast out for the common case — no maps registered at all, so the per-render-pass
+// lookup can be skipped entirely without taking the registry lock.
+bool AnyRasterizationRateMapsRegistered();
+
+// KL_ANGLE_VRR_TRACE=1. Read once: this is consulted on a per-render-pass path.
+bool RasterizationRateTraceEnabled();
